  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Cloudy, showery, and cool conditions continue \nthrough much of the day today, with drier conditions building \nback in for Thursday and Friday.\n\nAs of 330 AM, radar imagery shows steady rain in place for most\nlocations east of the Blue Ridge/south of I-70. Conditions have\nlargely dried out to the west of the Blue Ridge, other than some\nspotty drizzle. To the north of I-70/east of the Blue Ridge the\nprecipitation shield is a bit more broken up/showery in nature.\n\nA strong cold-air damming (CAD) wedge remains in place across \nthe entire area along with the aforementioned rain, cloudy \nskies, and cool temperatures. An unseasonably strong area of \nhigh pressure centered to our north across southern Quebec will \nreach peak strength today. As this occurs, drier air will slowly\nstart to filter into the area from the northeast. This drier \nair will slowly start to break down the precipitation shield, \nwith rain gradually coming to an end from northeast to \nsouthwest over the course of the day. Locations in central \nVirginia will be the last to see the precipitation/low clouds \nbreak up later tonight. Even after the precipitation comes to \nan end, it will still be a cool day with cloudy skies in place. \nTemperatures are only forecast to make it into the 50s to near \n60 to the west of the Blue Ridge, and then low to mid 60s to the\neast of the Blue Ridge. Winds will remain gusty out of the \nnortheast, with gusts to around 20-30 mph possible at times to \nthe east of the Blue Ridge.\n\nDrier conditions are forecast for Thursday and Friday as the \nupper trough axis that has been stalled out to our west finally \nstarts to make some eastward progress. This trough axis will \npass overhead tomorrow, and then progress offshore on Friday. As\nthis occurs, a coastal low will deepen off to our east in \nresponse. The pressure gradient between the high to our north \nand the developing coastal low will keep winds gusty out of the \nnorth to northeast. Temperatures will stay below normal as a \nresult, with highs in the 60s tomorrow, and then upper 60s to \nnear 70 on Friday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Breezy and warmer this weekend, but forecast remains \nuncertain as Nor'easter impacts Northeast U.S. \n\nAnomalously strong surface ridge (by late September standards) \nbegins to weaken this weekend as a strong Nor'easter develops off \nthe coast of Southern New England/LI. Ensemble members still show \nquite a bit of spread regarding the exact track of the Nor'easter. A \nfew members show the possibility the low tracks closer to the NJ / \nDelmarva coast this weekend. Regardless of exact position, we are \nlikely to be on the drier side of the system with northwest winds \nbringing in breezy, warmer, and mostly dry conditions. If the \nNor'easter tracks closer to our area that could bring some rain \nchances to the area, especially along/east of I-95, along with the \npotential for increasing winds. Safe to say that there isn't a \ndefinite trend for this weekend, so be sure to keep track of the \nforecast for any changes in the coming days.\n\nThe Nor'easter is eventually pushed offshore at the start of next \nweek as high pressure builds in from the OH Valley and Great Lakes. \nMid-level ridging builds over much of the eastern CONUS during the \nmiddle of next week, marking the return of much warmer temperatures \nand continued dry conditions.",
